   "texts": {
    "profile": "## Hola: the all-day greeting\n- Picture this: a new person hears hola, which means hello.\n- Layout: hola → any time of day → friends or someone just met.\n- Takeaway: hola is your all-day greeting.\n\n## Greetings by time of day\n- Next, buenos días means good morning.\n- Buenas tardes means good afternoon.\n- Buenas noches means good evening or good night.\n- Think of it like a game menu: each greeting fits its own time of day.\n- Takeaway: días is masculine plural; tardes and noches are feminine plural.\n\n## Saying goodbye\n- Then adiós means goodbye.\n- Hasta luego means see you later.\n- Think of it like a comic's next panel: you expect to meet again.\n- Takeaway: hasta luego fits when you expect to meet again soon.\n\n## Asking how someone is\n- Next, cómo estás is the informal how are you.\n- Think of it like a basketball team: teammates get estás, referees get usted.\n- Cómo está usted is the formal how are you.\n- Answer with bien, gracias, which means fine, thank you.\n- Takeaway: friends get estás; strangers and older people get usted.\n\n## Meeting someone new\n- Last, mucho gusto means nice to meet you.\n- Think of it like your comic's first panel: a new character gets mucho gusto.\n- Takeaway: mucho gusto marks a first meeting.",
    "mirror": "## Hola: the simplest greeting\n- Which greeting can you use at any time of day, with friends or with people you have just met?\n- Hola means hello, which is the simplest Spanish greeting and works at any hour of the day.\n\n## Greetings for a time of day\n- Spanish speakers say buenos días for good morning, buenas tardes for good afternoon, and buenas noches for good evening or good night.\n- Why is it buenos días but buenas tardes, when both greetings name a time of day?\n- Notice that días is masculine plural while tardes and noches are feminine plural, so buenos and buenas must take matching endings.\n- Layout: buenos días → buenos, buenas tardes → buenas, buenas noches → buenas, because tardes and noches are feminine plural.\n\n## Saying goodbye\n- Which goodbye phrase is friendly, and when would you use it rather than adiós?\n- Hasta luego means see you later, so it is friendly when you expect to see the person again soon.\n- Adiós means goodbye, which is the other option, because the text gives adiós and hasta luego for saying goodbye.\n\n## Asking how someone is\n- With friends, family and children you use cómo estás, which is the informal question that means how are you.\n- With older people, strangers and polite situations you use cómo está usted, which is formal and also means how are you.\n- The usual answer is bien, gracias, which means fine, thank you and fits both the informal and formal questions.\n\n## Meeting someone new\n- When you meet someone for the first time you say mucho gusto, which means nice to meet you.\n- Optional harder question: which greeting fits an afternoon with a stranger, and which question then asks how they are?"
   }
