  {
   "profile": "J3",
   "kind": "notes",
   "texts": {
    "profile": "## Militarism: the arms build-up before 1914\n- Historians often describe four long-term causes of the First World War, plus one event that set it off.\n- The first cause is militarism: before 1914 the great powers built up large armies and navies, and Germany and Britain raced to build battleships.\n- Think of it like two players in a video game who spend every resource on upgrading their fleets, just as Germany and Britain raced to build battleships.\n- Generals began to plan for war, and many leaders came to see war as a normal way to settle disputes.\n\n## Alliances: two blocs that widened any quarrel\n- The second cause is alliances, and Europe was divided into two blocs.\n- The Triple Entente joined France, Russia and Britain, and the Triple Alliance joined Germany, Austria-Hungary and Italy.\n- Because of these alliances, a local conflict could pull in many countries, so a quarrel between two states could grow into a continental war.\n- Think of it like a basketball game in which every teammate must step in when one player is challenged, just as the alliances turned a quarrel between two states into a continental war.\n\n## Imperialism: rivalry between empires\n- The third cause is imperialism, which means rivalry between empires for colonies, markets and power.\n- The European empires competed for land and resources in Africa and Asia, which increased distrust between them.\n\n## Nationalism: pride and the wish for independence\n- The fourth cause is nationalism, strong pride in one's nation or a wish for independence.\n- Nationalism was especially powerful in the Balkans, where Serbia wanted to unite the Slavic peoples and Austria-Hungary feared losing its empire.\n- Think of it like a comic artist who insists that every panel carry the artist's own style, much as nationalism is strong pride in one's nation.\n\n## The spark: from Sarajevo to a continental war\n- The spark was the assassination of Archduke Franz Ferdinand, heir to the Austro-Hungarian throne, in Sarajevo on 28 June 1914.\n- Austria-Hungary then went to war on Serbia, the step that followed from the assassination acting as the spark.\n- Because of the alliances, that war on Serbia soon drew in Russia, Germany, France and Britain.\n- The result is checked the way the material states it: the local quarrel grew into a continental war.\n- A harder question the material answers: which cause explains why a quarrel between two states could become a continental war? The answer is alliances.",
    "mirror": "## Militarism: the first cause\n- Historians often describe four long-term causes of the First World War, plus one event that set it off.\n- Militarism is the first cause: the great powers built up large armies and navies before 1914.\n- Germany and Britain raced each other to build battleships.\n- Generals began to plan for war.\n- Many leaders came to see war as a normal way to settle disputes.\n- Layout: armies and navies built up → battleship race (Germany vs Britain) → generals plan for war → leaders treat war as normal.\n\n## Alliances: two blocs\n- The second cause is alliances, and Europe was split into two blocs.\n- The Triple Entente joined France, Russia and Britain.\n- The Triple Alliance joined Germany, Austria-Hungary and Italy.\n- Because of these alliances, a local conflict could pull in many countries.\n- Think of it like a basketball scuffle — one foul brings both benches in, just as a local conflict pulled in many countries.\n- So a quarrel between two states could grow into a continental war.\n- Layout: Triple Entente = France + Russia + Britain | Triple Alliance = Germany + Austria-Hungary + Italy.\n\n## Imperialism: rivalry between empires\n- The third cause is imperialism, which means rivalry between empires for colonies, markets and power.\n- The European empires competed for land and resources in Africa and Asia.\n- Think of it like two players farming the same map for loot, much as the empires competed for land and resources in Africa and Asia.\n- That competition increased distrust between the empires.\n\n## Nationalism: pride and the Balkans\n- The fourth cause is nationalism: strong pride in your nation, or a wish for independence.\n- Nationalism was especially powerful in the Balkans.\n- In the Balkans, Serbia wanted to unite the Slavic peoples.\n- Think of it like a comic artist who wants their own series, much as Serbia wanted to unite the Slavic peoples.\n- Austria-Hungary feared it would lose its empire.\n- Layout: Balkans — Serbia wants to unite the Slavic peoples | Austria-Hungary fears losing its empire.\n\n## The spark: Sarajevo, 28 June 1914\n- The spark was the assassination of Archduke Franz Ferdinand, heir to the Austro-Hungarian throne.\n- He was shot in Sarajevo on 28 June 1914, and Austria-Hungary's war on Serbia then drew in Russia, Germany, France and Britain.\n- Layout: Franz Ferdinand shot in Sarajevo (28 June 1914) → Austria-Hungary at war with Serbia → Russia, Germany, France and Britain drawn in."
   }
